<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Reforma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1855">1855</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Reform era</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Reforma was a period of liberal reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> that began after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-of-ayutla">Revolution of Ayutla</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> in 1855. Laws such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ley-juarez">Ley Juárez</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ley-lerdo">Ley Lerdo</a> curtailed the legal privileges of the Church and the army and forced the sale of corporate lands, and the Constitution of 1857 enshrined these principles. Conservative resistance led to the Reform War of 1858–1861 and helped prepare the way for French intervention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-reforma.jpg" alt="La Reforma" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petronilo Monroy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alegor%C3%ADa_de_la_Constituci%C3%B3n_de_1857.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Similar measures had been tried under the liberal president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentin-gomez-farias">Valentín Gómez Farías</a>, who took power in April 1833, closed church schools and monasteries and claimed the right to make church appointments, until a military coup removed him in April 1835. Under the Plan of Ayutla a government led by Juan Álvarez took office in November 1855, with a cabinet including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a>, Miguel Lerdo de Tejada, Melchor Ocampo, Guillermo Prieto and the more moderate Ignacio Comonfort. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ley-juarez">Ley Juárez</a> of 23 November 1855 ended the jurisdiction of military and church courts over purely civil cases, and the Ley Lafragua of December 1855 allowed freedom of the press.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Comonfort replaced Álvarez as president in December 1855. A constituent congress met on 14 February 1856, the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was suppressed on 5 June and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ley-lerdo">Ley Lerdo</a> was promulgated on 25 June, forcing church and civil corporations, including Indigenous communities, to sell their land to private buyers. A civil registry of births, marriages and deaths followed on 27 January 1857, and the Constitution of 1857, promulgated on 5 February, created a single-chamber congress and guaranteed freedoms of speech, press, assembly and education.</p><p>On 17 December 1857 General Félix María Zuloaga proclaimed the Plan of Tacubaya, which Comonfort accepted before resigning and leaving the country in January 1858. The presidency passed to Juárez, president of the Supreme Court, while conservatives elected Zuloaga. From Veracruz Juárez issued the nationalization of church property on 12 July 1859 and the civil marriage law on 23 July 1859. After the liberal victory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded in 1862, and the emperor Maximilian, invited by conservatives, in fact ratified the Reform Laws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Reform separated church and state and committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to free, compulsory, public and secular education, though schools could be built only after the wars. The forced sale of communal lands largely failed: Indigenous villagers lacked the capital to buy, and the land passed mostly to well-off non-Indigenous buyers and large estate owners. The church lost its charitable role, which the state did not take up. The Constitution of 1857 lasted until 1917, and historians judge that the liberals built a constitutional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> under Juárez, Lerdo and Díaz rather than the democracy they had hoped for.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Reforma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The liberal Revolution of Ayutla removed him in 1855 and launched <b>La Reforma</b>: the Ley Juárez and Ley Lerdo curbed church and corporate privileges, and the Constitution of 1857 followed.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-federal-republic-of-mexico">Second Federal Republic of Mexico</a></li></ul></section>
